---
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';

const title = 'mp-units vs physure vs Sounio: correlated uncertainty';
const description = 'Which tool handles x - x = 0? A sourced comparison of Sounio, mp-units (C++) and physure (Python/Rust) for units, correlated uncertainty and static checks.';
const heading = 'Sounio vs mp-units vs physure: units and correlated uncertainty compared';

const author = {
  '@type': 'Person',
  name: 'Demetrios Chiuratto Agourakis',
  url: 'https://www.souniolang.org/about/',
  sameAs: [
    'https://orcid.org/0009-0001-8671-8878',
    'https://github.com/agourakis82',
  ],
};

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Learn', href: '/learn/' },
  { name: 'Epistemic', href: '/learn/epistemic/' },
  { name: 'Sounio vs mp-units vs physure', href: '/learn/epistemic/sounio-vs-mp-units-vs-physure/' },
];

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Which tool knows that x − x has zero uncertainty?',
    a: 'mp-units does not: its uncertainty guide says it does not track correlations, so x - x reports a non-zero uncertainty. physure does when the two sides are the same value: x - x printed 0.0 m, while two separately constructed quantities with the same numbers printed a non-zero uncertainty. Sounio does: h - h prints variance 0, and correlate(a, b, rho) changes the variance of two different inputs. Epsil’s examples page does not document correlation for measurements.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Are unit errors caught at compile time?',
    a: 'mp-units: g++ exited 2 on metres added to seconds. Sounio: souc check exits 1 with error[E041] on the same kind of addition. physure: the Python package we ran raised IncompatibleUnitsError at run time; the README also describes a mypy plugin, which was not run. Epsil’s examples page documents UnitConvert, not a refusal before the program runs.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can one value carry both a unit and its uncertainty?',
    a: 'In Sounio, yes. Knowledge<m> holds 100.0 m and standard uncertainty 0.5 m together, and the quotient of that value with a Knowledge<s> is stored as velocity. The program printed speed=10.000000, variance=0.012500, std_unc=0.111803.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which propagation methods are supported?',
    a: 'First-order GUM in Sounio, mp-units and physure. physure’s README also names Monte Carlo and the Unscented Transform. Monte Carlo is not claimed for Sounio.',
  },
  {
    q: 'When should I not choose Sounio?',
    a: 'Choose physure for a Python or Rust codebase that already wants covariance by default. Choose mp-units for an existing C++ codebase. Pint, with the uncertainties package, stays on the units guide’s comparison table; this page does not repeat that table.',
  },
];

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faq.map((item) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: item.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: item.a },
  })),
};

const headings = [
  { id: 'x-minus-x', text: 'Which tool knows that x − x has zero uncertainty?', depth: 2 },
  { id: 'unit-errors', text: 'Are unit errors caught at compile time?', depth: 2 },
  { id: 'one-value', text: 'Can one value carry both a unit and its uncertainty?', depth: 2 },
  { id: 'propagation', text: 'Which propagation methods are supported?', depth: 2 },
  { id: 'three-tests', text: 'Same three tests in each tool', depth: 2 },
  { id: 'limits', text: 'Known limits and maturity', depth: 2 },
  { id: 'when-not', text: 'When not to choose Sounio', depth: 2 },
  { id: 'faq', text: 'FAQ', depth: 2 },
];

const sounioOne = `let distance: Knowledge<m> = measure(100.0, uncertainty: 0.5)
let elapsed: Knowledge<s> = measure(10.0, uncertainty: 0.1)
let speed: velocity = distance.value / elapsed.value
let variance = variance_of(speed)`;

const sounioLengths = `let left: Knowledge<m> = measure(10.0, uncertainty: 0.2)
let right: Knowledge<m> = measure(4.0, uncertainty: 0.1)
let a = left.value
let b = right.value
let v_ind = variance_of(a + b)
let _r = correlate(a, b, 1.0)
let v_cor = variance_of(a + b)`;

const mpUnits = `#include <iostream>
#include <mp-units/systems/si.h>
#include <mp-units/utility/uncertain.h>

using namespace mp_units;
using namespace mp_units::si::unit_symbols;
using mp_units::utility::uncertain;

int main() {
  quantity distance = uncertain{100.0, 0.5} * m;
  quantity elapsed = uncertain{10.0, 0.1} * s;
  quantity speed = distance / elapsed;
  std::cout << speed << "\\n";
  return 0;
}`;

const physure = `from physure import Q_

distance = Q_(100.0, "m", uncertainty=0.5)
elapsed = Q_(10.0, "s", uncertainty=0.1)
print((distance / elapsed).to("m/s"))
print(distance + elapsed)`;
---

<DocsLayout title={title} description={description} heading={heading} headings={headings} schema={schema} localizedAlternates={false} breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs} articleHeadline={heading} articleAuthor={author}>
  <p><strong>In short</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>mp-units for unit safety in C++.</li>
    <li>physure for Python or Rust, with covariance by default.</li>
    <li>Sounio when one value should carry a unit and a correlated uncertainty, and metres added to seconds is refused before the program runs.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Pint, with the uncertainties package, is already in the <a href="/learn/language/units-and-uncertainty/">units and uncertainty</a> table (F#, Ada, Numbat, Pint, Unitful). This page does not repeat that table. The language overview is <a href="/language/">/language/</a>. Epsil is the fourth tool below, from its public examples, and it was not executed here.
  </p>

  <h2 id="x-minus-x">Which tool knows that x − x has zero uncertainty?</h2>
  <p>
    mp-units does not. physure does, by default. Sounio does, both when one value is used twice and when two different inputs are joined with <code>correlate</code>. Epsil’s examples do not document it for measurements.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="https://mpusz.github.io/mp-units/HEAD/how_to_guides/advanced_usage/working_with_measurement_uncertainty/">mp-units’ uncertainty guide</a> says propagation assumes independent values, so <code>x - x</code> keeps a non-zero uncertainty, and that full correlation is out of scope. That subtraction was not re-run here.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="https://github.com/Alexisrx96/physure">physure’s README</a> says correlated mode is the default, with a sparse covariance, and that independent errors make <code>x - x</code> report σ·√2 instead of zero. With physure 0.2.4, one quantity minus itself printed <code>0.0 m</code>. Two separately constructed <code>Q_(10.0, "m", uncertainty=0.2)</code> values subtracted to <code>(0.0 ± 0.28284271247461906) m</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Sounio: full covariance between any inputs. Same-variable correlation is automatic. <a href="https://github.com/Sounio-lang/sounio/blob/main/tests/run-pass/gum_correlated.sio"><code>tests/run-pass/gum_correlated.sio</code></a> prints <code>var(h-h)=0.000000</code> and <code>var(h+h)=0.000400</code> (four times <code>var(h)</code>, not twice). Two different inputs use <code>correlate(a, b, rho)</code>. <a href="https://github.com/Sounio-lang/sounio/blob/main/tests/run-pass/madaros_gum_fo_correlate.sio"><code>tests/run-pass/madaros_gum_fo_correlate.sio</code></a> measures <code>x</code> as 1.0±0.1 and <code>y</code> as 2.0±0.2 and prints <code>v_ind=0.050000</code>, then <code>v_cor=0.090000</code> after <code>correlate(x, y, 1.0)</code>. The same shape on two <code>Knowledge&lt;m&gt;</code> values is <code>tests/run-pass/gum_unit_correlate_two_lengths.sio</code> (added in this pull request):
  </p>
  <pre><code>{sounioLengths}</code></pre>
  <pre><code>{`v_ind=0.050000
v_cor=0.090000
var_same=0.000000
UNIT_CORRELATE_PASS`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    A correlation declared in one function does not leak into the next. <a href="https://github.com/Sounio-lang/sounio/blob/main/tests/run-pass/madaros_gum_correlate_is_per_function.sio"><code>tests/run-pass/madaros_gum_correlate_is_per_function.sio</code></a> prints <code>v_cor=0.160000</code> and <code>v_ind=0.100000</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="https://epsil.dev/examples/#units-and-measurements">Epsil’s examples</a> document <code>measurement(value, error)</code> propagating in quadrature, and symbolic simplification of expressions. The word correlation does not appear on that page. Correlation for measurements is not documented there.
  </p>

  <h2 id="unit-errors">Are unit errors caught at compile time?</h2>
  <p>
    mp-units and Sounio refuse the addition before the program runs. physure’s Python package refuses when the addition runs; its README also describes an optional mypy plugin. Epsil’s examples show conversion, not a refusal before execution.
  </p>
  <p>
    mp-units, commit <a href="https://github.com/mpusz/mp-units/commit/5e34599"><code>5e34599</code></a> (CMake project version 2.6.0), g++ 13.3.0, <code>-std=c++20</code>. A second translation unit kept the two <code>uncertain</code> quantities below and added <code>quantity bad = distance + elapsed;</code>. g++ exited 2. The first diagnostic was <code>no match for ‘operator+’</code> between <code>quantity&lt;metre, uncertain&lt;double&gt;&gt;</code> and <code>quantity&lt;second, uncertain&lt;double&gt;&gt;</code>. The <a href="https://mpusz.github.io/mp-units/HEAD/getting_started/safety_features/">safety guide</a> describes that refusal as “cannot add length and time”.
  </p>
  <p>
    Sounio: <code>examples/units/metres_plus_seconds.sio</code> adds <code>Knowledge&lt;m&gt;</code> to <code>Knowledge&lt;s&gt;</code>. <code>./bin/souc check</code> exits 1:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`error[E041]: unit mismatch in arithmetic operation
  left unit: length^1
  right unit: time^1
error[E000]: type checking failed`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    physure 0.2.4, on <code>distance + elapsed</code> in the listing below, raised <code>IncompatibleUnitsError: Cannot operate with incompatible units: 'm' and 's'.</code> That is a Python exception at run time. The README’s mypy plugin was not run.
  </p>
  <p>
    Epsil: <a href="https://epsil.dev/examples/#units-and-measurements">the examples page</a> shows <code>UnitConvert</code> of 30 km/h into m/s. Those pages do not show a unit mismatch refused before the program runs. <code>isCompatibleUnit</code> was not found on the homepage or the examples page, so it is not claimed here.
  </p>

  <h2 id="one-value">Can one value carry both a unit and its uncertainty?</h2>
  <p>
    In Sounio, yes. One <code>Knowledge&lt;m&gt;</code> is the distance and its standard uncertainty. Dividing by <code>Knowledge&lt;s&gt;</code> stores a <code>velocity</code> (<code>unit velocity = m / s</code> in the same file).
  </p>
  <pre><code>{sounioOne}</code></pre>
  <p>
    The file is <code>examples/units/velocity_with_uncertainty.sio</code> (it also has a local square root, <code>sqrt_f64</code>). Its stdout:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`speed=10.000000
variance=0.012500
std_unc=0.111803`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    <code>print_f64</code> stops at six digits, so 0.111803 is the printed standard uncertainty of √0.0125. The two inputs are independent in this file. The length pair above is the one that calls <code>correlate</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    mp-units puts the uncertainty in <code>uncertain&#123;value, sigma&#125;</code> and the unit in the quantity type (<code>uncertain&#123;100.0, 0.5&#125; * m</code>), as in the <a href="https://mpusz.github.io/mp-units/HEAD/how_to_guides/advanced_usage/working_with_measurement_uncertainty/">uncertainty guide</a>. physure puts both in <code>Q_(100.0, "m", uncertainty=0.5)</code>, the constructor in its README. Those are library values in C++ and Python. The Sounio value above is what the program in this repository printed.
  </p>

  <h2 id="propagation">Which propagation methods are supported?</h2>
  <p>
    First-order GUM in Sounio, mp-units and physure. physure’s README adds Monte Carlo and the Unscented Transform. Monte Carlo is not claimed for Sounio.
  </p>
  <p>
    Sounio’s velocity file is the independent first-order law (JCGM 100:2008 §5.1.2): variance 0.012500. The correlated numbers are the <code>correlate</code> tests above, not a Monte Carlo sample. The <a href="https://mpusz.github.io/mp-units/HEAD/how_to_guides/advanced_usage/working_with_measurement_uncertainty/">mp-units guide</a> documents first-order formulas for independent values. <a href="https://github.com/Alexisrx96/physure">physure’s README</a> names Gaussian (first-order), Monte Carlo and Unscented Transform. Only the default Python path was run. Epsil’s examples say <code>measurement</code> propagates in quadrature; <code>N(measurement(10, 0.1) * measurement(20, 0.2))</code> is shown as <code>200.0 ± 2.8</code>. That product was not re-run.
  </p>

  <h2 id="three-tests">Same three tests in each tool</h2>
  <p>
    The three cases are a unit mismatch, x − x, and BMI = m/h². A cell is filled only when this repository ran it or the cited page states it.
  </p>
  <div class="compare-wrap">
    <table class="compare-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th></th>
          <th>Unit mismatch</th>
          <th>x − x</th>
          <th>BMI = m/h²</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th>Sounio</th>
          <td><code>error[E041]</code>, exit 1, on <code>examples/units/metres_plus_seconds.sio</code>.</td>
          <td><code>var(h-h)=0.000000</code> from <code>gum_correlated.sio</code>.</td>
          <td><code>examples/science/uncertainty_propagation.sio</code> printed <code>BMI = 23.148148 +/- 0.299946 kg/m^2</code>. The file’s own check accepts that as ~23.148 ± 0.300. It is a <code>Measurement</code> struct and hand-written partials; the unit in the print is a string. The <code>Knowledge&lt;m&gt;</code> value is the velocity file, not this one.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>mp-units</th>
          <td>g++ exit 2, <code>no match for operator+</code>, on the two <code>uncertain</code> quantities. The division program printed <code>10 ± 0.111803 m/s</code>.</td>
          <td>Not re-run. The uncertainty guide says a non-zero uncertainty.</td>
          <td>Not in the guide cited here, and not run.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>physure</th>
          <td><code>IncompatibleUnitsError</code> at run time, physure 0.2.4. The division printed <code>(10.0 ± 0.1118033988749895) m/s</code>.</td>
          <td>One quantity minus itself printed <code>0.0 m</code>. Two separately constructed quantities with the same numbers printed <code>(0.0 ± 0.28284271247461906) m</code>.</td>
          <td>Not in the README sections cited here, and not run.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Epsil</th>
          <td>Not shown on the examples page. Not run.</td>
          <td>Not documented for measurements on that page. Not run.</td>
          <td>Not shown there. The documented product is <code>200.0 ± 2.8</code> for 10±0.1 by 20±0.2.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <pre><code>{mpUnits}</code></pre>
  <pre><code>{physure}</code></pre>

  <h2 id="limits">Known limits and maturity</h2>
  <p>
    Sounio is pre-1.0 and <a href="https://github.com/Sounio-lang/sounio/blob/main/LICENSE">Apache-2.0</a>. physure is <a href="https://github.com/Alexisrx96/physure/blob/master/LICENSE">MIT</a> (README: Irvin Torres). mp-units is <a href="https://github.com/mpusz/mp-units/blob/master/LICENSE.md">MIT</a>. Linux x86-64 is the Sounio target this page was run on.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="https://github.com/Sounio-lang/sounio/blob/main/docs/compiler/KNOWN_LIMITATIONS.md">KL-11</a> is not closed. A pure helper carries first-order channels across a call, and that section records further shapes that carry them too. <a href="https://github.com/Sounio-lang/sounio/blob/main/docs/compiler/KNOWN_LIMITATIONS.md">KL-15</a>: no <code>Knowledge&lt;f128&gt;</code> and no GUM over <code>f128</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>tests/compile-fail/correlate_rho_must_be_literal.sio</code> expects <code>error[E243]</code> when ρ is not a literal. On the compiler used for this page, <code>./bin/souc run</code> of that file compiled and printed <code>unreachable</code>. The page does not say that a runtime ρ is refused. <code>lower_correlate_call_ref</code> in <code>self-hosted/ir/lower.sio</code> lowers a non-literal ρ into a register.
  </p>
  <p>
    physure’s README states a mypy plugin, JVM and PHS bindings, and timings. The plugin was not run, the other bindings were not run, and the timings were not remeasured. mp-units’ guide also documents unit conversion (<code>length.in(mm)</code>). The Sounio programs on this page do not convert kilometres to metres.
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`export SOUNIO_STDLIB_PATH="$(pwd)/stdlib"
./bin/souc run examples/units/velocity_with_uncertainty.sio
./bin/souc run tests/run-pass/gum_unit_correlate_two_lengths.sio
./bin/souc run tests/run-pass/gum_correlated.sio
./bin/souc check examples/units/metres_plus_seconds.sio`}</code></pre>
  <p>The check is supposed to exit 1.</p>

  <h2 id="when-not">When not to choose Sounio</h2>
  <p>
    Choose physure when the rest of the work is Python or Rust and covariance-by-default is the point. Choose mp-units when the program is already C++. Sounio does not replace either library’s ecosystem.
  </p>
  <p>
    Pint remains the Python units library in the <a href="/learn/language/units-and-uncertainty/">units guide</a>. This page does not restate that row.
  </p>

  <h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
  {faq.map((item) => (
    <div>
      <h3>{item.q}</h3>
      <p>{item.a}</p>
    </div>
  ))}
</DocsLayout>

